#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167896} :activity/id #uuid "797cab7f-b62f-590f-9f20-4093fac9d314" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/id #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" :topic/math-academy-id 3187} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167991 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of ordinary differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) =\nA\n\\mathbf{x}(t) +\n\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix and $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is a forcing vector.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll derive the formula for the particular solution of the above system using the method of variation of parameters.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_c(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}$ is a vector of constants, and $\\Phi$ is the fundamental matrix.\n\nAs usual, for the method of variation of parameters, we'll look for a particular solution of our inhomogeneous system by varying the constant parameters, i.e., in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined.\n\nDifferentiating $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{'}(t) & =(Φ\\,u(t))^{'} \\\\  & =Φ{u}^{'}(t)+{Φ}^{'}u(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a solution, substituting it back into the initial system gives a true equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{'}(t)+{Φ}^{'}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, $\\Phi' = A \\Phi.$ Substituting this into the equation simplifies it to the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{'}(t)+AΦu(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{'}(t) & =f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\Phi$ is invertible, we can pre-multiply both sides by $\\Phi^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{'}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate both sides of the final equations (ignoring the constant term):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\displaystyle\\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "99630576-f78f-593a-af83-87c98421e2c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14396 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "94760db8-7585-5363-8838-c0f44735b806" :step/math-academy-id 592305 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166524 :question/id #uuid "6060e2fe-05b3-587d-b5dd-3cdab705eb05" :question/math-academy-id "e-21546"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396972}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "4a088561-5bc3-556f-b5d5-470901c2c1cf" :step/math-academy-id 592306 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166525 :question/id #uuid "1d203940-c05d-5f30-a8ad-c618d6d6ba5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21547"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365803}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "387d38d2-d1da-59ba-b0d2-529bed76253e" :step/math-academy-id 592309 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166526 :question/id #uuid "ce663953-3e5e-55e2-a8db-6d7a531496cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365808}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "9a3cf6ac-30bf-5301-9c2b-c2870c934a65" :step/math-academy-id 592312}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166985}]}
